Now, looking at the hyperparameters that vary across trials: ad0-ad14 (which are the number of nodes added to each class), dim (dimensionality), layers, lr (learning rate), num_K (number of clusters), rbo (weight for similarity rankings), rounds (iterations in UNREAL), threshold (distance filter), and factor (scaling for node addition).

I notice that trials with higher test accuracies tend to have lower ad0 values. For example, trial 9 has ad0=4 and achieves the highest accuracy. Maybe adding fewer nodes to class 0 helps balance the classes better without overfitting.

The dimensionality seems to be mostly 256 across top performers, but trial 19 uses 128 and still does well. So maybe 256 is a good default, but lower dimensions could also work depending on other parameters.

